    "q-339624": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339624",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{7}-3{x}^{3}-4}{2{x}^{5}+7}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{7}-3{x}^{3}-4}{2{x}^{5}+7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(6{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{6{x}^{2}-0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
